.TH IOCOST_COEF_GEN 8 2026-09-21 "Linux 7.0.9" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
iocost_coef_gen \- generate blk-iocost linear cost model coefficients for a disk
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B iocost_coef_gen
.RB \%[ \-\-testdev
.IR dev ]
.RB \%[ \-\-testfile\-size\-gb
.IR gigabytes ]
.RB \%[ \-\-duration
.IR seconds ]
.RB \%[ \-\-seqio\-block\-mb
.IR megabytes ]
.RB \%[ \-\-seq\-depth
.IR depth ]
.RB \%[ \-\-rand\-depth
.IR depth ]
.RB \%[ \-\-numjobs
.IR jobs ]
.RB \%[ \-\-quiet ]
.RB \%[ \-\-verbose ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B iocost_coef_gen
measures a disk with
.BR fio (1)
and prints coefficients for the linear I/O cost model of the blk-iocost
controller, in the format accepted by the
.I io.cost.model
file of the root cgroup.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-testdev ,
the tests run against the raw block device named by
.IR dev .
Otherwise they run against the file
.I iocost\-coef\-fio.testfile
in the current working directory.
In that case the program asks
.BR findmnt (8)
for the source device of the filesystem that contains the current
directory and, if that device is a partition, uses the disk that
contains it.
If a regular file named
.I iocost\-coef\-fio.testfile
of exactly the requested size already exists, it is reused.
Otherwise any existing file of that name is removed, a new empty file is
created,
.B chattr +C
is run on it (a failure of this step is ignored),
and it is filled with data read from
.I /dev/urandom
through
.BR pv (1)
and
.BR dd (1),
using direct I/O.
The test file is not removed when the program finishes.
.PP
Before testing, the program records the disk's active I/O scheduler and
its
.I nomerges
setting, then sets the scheduler to
.B none
and
.I nomerges
to
.BR 1 .
It restores both when the tests finish.
.PP
It then runs six
.BR fio (1)
tests in the following order.
Each runs for the time set by
.BR \-\-duration ,
uses direct I/O and the
.B libaio
engine, and runs the number of parallel jobs set by
.BR \-\-numjobs .
.TP
.B rbps
Sequential read, block size set by
.BR \-\-seqio\-block\-mb ,
queue depth 1.
.TP
.B rseqiops
Sequential read, block size 4096 bytes, queue depth set by
.BR \-\-seq\-depth .
.TP
.B rrandiops
Random read, block size 4096 bytes, queue depth set by
.BR \-\-rand\-depth .
.TP
.B wbps
Sequential write, block size set by
.BR \-\-seqio\-block\-mb ,
queue depth 1.
.TP
.B wseqiops
Sequential write, block size 4096 bytes, queue depth set by
.BR \-\-seq\-depth .
.TP
.B wrandiops
Random write, block size 4096 bytes, queue depth set by
.BR \-\-rand\-depth .
.PP
Each
.B bps
value is the measured throughput in bytes per second, summed over all
jobs.
Each
.B iops
value is the measured throughput divided by 4096 and rounded to the
nearest integer.
.PP
The result is printed to standard output as a single line:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
\fImaj\fP:\fImin\fP rbps=\fIn\fP rseqiops=\fIn\fP rrandiops=\fIn\fP wbps=\fIn\fP wseqiops=\fIn\fP wrandiops=\fIn\fP
.EE
.RE
.PP
where
.IR maj : min
is the device number of the tested disk.
Unless
.B \-\-quiet
is given, progress messages are printed to standard output before this
line.
.PP
.B Warning:
with
.BR \-\-testdev ,
.BR fio (1)
is given the device node itself as its target, with no size or offset
limit, and the
.BR wbps ,
.B wseqiops
and
.B wrandiops
tests each write to it for the time set by
.BR \-\-duration .
The program's own help text describes these as destructive tests
performed against the whole device.
Treat all data on the device, including any partition table and
filesystems, as destroyed.
The program asks for no confirmation and does not check whether the
device is mounted or in use.
Without
.BR \-\-testdev ,
the write tests overwrite only the contents of the test file.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage message and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-testdev " dev"
Run the tests against the block device
.IR /dev/name ,
where
.I name
is the last path component of
.I dev
(so
.B sdb
and
.B /dev/sdb
are equivalent).
The queue settings are looked up under
.IR /sys/block/name ,
so
.I name
must be a whole disk, not a partition.
No test file is created and
.B \-\-testfile\-size\-gb
is ignored.
This destroys the data on the device; see the warning in DESCRIPTION.
.TP
.BI \-\-testfile\-size\-gb " gigabytes"
Size of the test file in gibibytes (units of 1073741824 bytes);
fractional values are accepted.
The default is 16.
.TP
.BI \-\-duration " seconds"
Duration of each of the six tests, as an integer number of seconds.
The default is 120.
.TP
.BI \-\-seqio\-block\-mb " megabytes"
Block size of the
.B rbps
and
.B wbps
tests, as an integer number of mebibytes (units of 1048576 bytes).
The default is 128.
.TP
.BI \-\-seq\-depth " depth"
Queue depth of the
.B rseqiops
and
.B wseqiops
tests.
The default is 64.
.TP
.BI \-\-rand\-depth " depth"
Queue depth of the
.B rrandiops
and
.B wrandiops
tests.
The default is 64.
.TP
.BI \-\-numjobs " jobs"
Number of parallel
.BR fio (1)
jobs in each test.
The default is 1.
The program's help text notes that on high-performance devices more
than one job is needed to saturate the device.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
Print only the result line on standard output.
Progress messages are suppressed,
.BR fio (1)
is run with its ETA display disabled, and
.BR pv (1)
is run with
.BR \-q .
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Print each
.BR fio (1)
command line before running it.
This has no effect when
.B \-\-quiet
is also given.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A required command is missing, or a step failed (for example, an
external command exited with a non-zero status or a file could not be
opened); the failure is reported as a Python traceback.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line arguments.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I iocost\-coef\-fio.testfile
Test file in the current working directory, used when
.B \-\-testdev
is not given.
It is created if needed and left in place afterwards.
.TP
.I /dev/name
The tested disk.
With
.BR \-\-testdev ,
it is the target of the tests; in both modes its device number is
obtained with
.BR stat (2).
.TP
.I /sys/block/*/name
Searched without
.B \-\-testdev
to find the disk that contains partition
.IR name .
.TP
.I /sys/block/name/queue/scheduler
Read to record the active I/O scheduler, set to
.B none
for the tests, and restored afterwards.
.TP
.I /sys/block/name/queue/nomerges
Read to record the current value, set to
.B 1
for the tests, and restored afterwards.
.TP
.I /dev/urandom
Source of the test file's contents.
.TP
A temporary file in \fB$TMPDIR\fP or \fI/tmp\fP
Receives the JSON output of each
.BR fio (1)
run; it is removed after the result has been read.
.SH NOTES
The program runs these external commands through
.IR /bin/sh :
.BR findmnt (8),
.BR rm (1),
.BR touch (1),
.BR chattr (1),
.BR pv (1),
.BR dd (1)
and
.BR fio (1).
At startup it checks that
.BR findmnt ,
.BR pv ,
.B dd
and
.B fio
are in
.BR PATH ,
and exits with status 1 if any is missing, even when
.B \-\-testdev
is given and only
.B fio
is used.
.PP
The queue attributes
.I scheduler
and
.I nomerges
are created by the kernel with mode 0644, so the program needs root
privileges to change them.
With
.BR \-\-testdev ,
it also needs read and write access to the device node.
.PP
The scheduler and
.I nomerges
changes apply to all I/O to the disk while the tests run, including I/O
to other partitions and filesystems on it.
The original values are restored when the program exits normally, when
it stops because of an error, and when it is interrupted with
.BR SIGINT .
They are not restored if the program is killed by a signal that Python
does not handle, such as
.B SIGTERM
or
.BR SIGKILL .
.PP
Without
.BR \-\-testdev ,
the disk is identified from the last path component of the filesystem
source that
.BR findmnt (8)
reports.
That name must be a disk or partition that exists under both
.I /dev
and
.IR /sys/block ;
otherwise the program fails before creating the test file.
.PP
With the default duration, the six tests take 12 minutes in total, not
counting creation of the test file.
.PP
.I io.cost.model
exists only in the root cgroup of the cgroup v2 hierarchy, and only
when the kernel is built with
.BR CONFIG_BLK_CGROUP_IOCOST .
According to
.I \%Documentation/\:admin\-guide/\:cgroup\-v2.rst
in the Linux source, writing model parameters to it sets the device's
.B ctrl
key to
.BR user ,
which disables automatic changes to the model by the kernel.
.PP
.B iocost_coef_gen
is a Python 3 script.
.SH EXAMPLES
Measure the disk that holds
.I /srv/scratch
with a 4 GiB test file and 60-second tests:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
# cd /srv/scratch
# iocost_coef_gen \-\-testfile\-size\-gb 4 \-\-duration 60
.EE
.RE
.PP
Measure the whole of
.IR /dev/sdb ,
destroying its contents, and load the result into the controller:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
# iocost_coef_gen \-\-quiet \-\-testdev /dev/sdb > coef
# cat coef > /sys/fs/cgroup/io.cost.model
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR chattr (1),
.BR dd (1),
.BR fio (1),
.BR pv (1),
.BR cgroups (7),
.BR findmnt (8)
.PP
In the Linux source:
.IP \(bu 3
.I \%Documentation/\:admin\-guide/\:cgroup\-v2.rst
(the io.cost.model and io.cost.qos entries)
.IP \(bu 3
.I \%block/\:blk\-iocost.c
(the controller implementation)
